<h2>The One Who Deleted and the One Who Rendered — Cherny's &ldquo;80% of the Harness&rdquo; and Karpathy's &ldquo;LOTR Movie&rdquo;</h2>

<p>In the two weeks after Opus 5 shipped, the two most-shared scenes in the AI world looked like opposites. <strong>Boris Cherny — creator of Claude Code</strong> — said in a talk that when the new model arrived, his team <strong>deleted more than 80% of the system prompt, and the model got smarter</strong>. <strong>Andrej Karpathy</strong> declared we are &ldquo;leaving the territory&rdquo; of testing LLMs with SVG pelicans, handed Opus 5 the first paragraph of The Lord of the Rings and a $10 budget, and got back <strong>a 3D animation that runs in the browser</strong>. One story is about erasing, the other about conjuring — and they are <strong>two faces of the same event</strong>. This article cross-verifies both against multiple reports, lays out the details, and analyzes why they are one story.</p>

