<h3>Three-line summary</h3>
<ul>
    <li>When Opus 5 arrived, Cherny's team <strong>deleted more than 80% of Claude Code's system prompt</strong> — and the model ran <strong>&ldquo;slightly smarter&rdquo;</strong>. The methodology is <strong>ablation</strong> (delete everything, reintroduce line by line, measure); the advice: &ldquo;<strong>sabotage yourselves — delete your system prompt every six months</strong>&rdquo;.</li>
    <li>Karpathy gave Opus 5 the <strong>first paragraph of LOTR plus a 1M-token budget (~$10)</strong> and asked for a three.js render — about two hours later it had written <strong>5,500 lines of code that procedurally render a 3D movie</strong>, <a href="https://karpathy.ai/lotr-movie/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">playable and forkable in the browser</a> (embedded below).</li>
    <li>The shared structure: as models improve, <strong>scaffolding gets deleted, output scale jumps, and the bottleneck moves to verification and perception</strong> — the weakness Karpathy flagged (&ldquo;LLMs can't watch their own video; they audit by slowly taking screenshots&rdquo;) is exactly that bottleneck.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Cherny — what &ldquo;we deleted 80% and it got smarter&rdquo; actually means</h3>
<p>Cherny created Claude Code and now heads it — so this is <strong>the harness's own author deleting the harness</strong>. Piecing the reports together:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>What was deleted</strong> — more than <strong>80%</strong> of Claude Code's system prompt when Opus 5 landed. Behaviors that older models had to be told about, the new model <strong>already does without being told</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>The result</strong> — performance actually improved: in Cherny's words the model became <strong>&ldquo;slightly smarter&rdquo;</strong>. Stale scaffolding is not neutral — it is <strong>a cost that fights the model</strong>. Instructions written to patch an old model's weaknesses become shackles once those weaknesses are gone.</li>
    <li><strong>The methodology: ablation</strong> — this was not a hunch. The team <strong>deletes the entire system prompt and reintroduces it line by line, measuring the impact of each line</strong>, for every model generation. When a new model arrives, the default posture is &ldquo;throw essentially everything away and start over&rdquo;.</li>
    <li><strong>The advice</strong> — generalized for everyone: <strong>&ldquo;sabotage yourselves — delete your system prompt every six months&rdquo;</strong>. A harness is not an asset; it is <strong>a consumable that depreciates</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>The next layer: loops</strong> — from the same talk lineage comes the line <strong>&ldquo;I don't prompt Claude anymore — I have loops that prompt Claude&rdquo;</strong>. The human job moves up from writing one good prompt to <strong>designing the loop</strong> in which the model observes, plans, acts and reflects for hours or days. The showcase: thousands of parallel agents <strong>rewriting the Bun runtime from Zig to Rust in 11 days</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Read through this wiki's <a href="#!hns-overview">harness engineering series</a> — which defines the harness as &ldquo;the whole system in which the AI works&rdquo; — Cherny's remarks are not the abolition of the harness but <strong>its generational turnover</strong>. What got deleted is the prompt layer, the fastest to go stale; the upper layers — loops, verification, tools — got thicker. The precise reading is <strong>&ldquo;the harness's center of gravity moved from instructions to structure&rdquo;</strong>.</p>

<h3>Karpathy — leaving the pelican for Middle-earth</h3>
<p>There is a lineage here. For years, the community's informal test for every new model was <strong>&ldquo;draw an SVG of a pelican riding a bicycle&rdquo;</strong> — making the model draw with code to probe its spatial and structural sense. In this post Karpathy declared <strong>&ldquo;we're starting to leave that territory&rdquo;</strong>, and showed the generalized version himself.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Input</strong>: the <strong>first paragraph</strong> of The Lord of the Rings + a <strong>1M-token budget (~$10)</strong> + a request to render it in three.js. That's all — no storyboard, no assets, no scene list.</li>
    <li><strong>Output</strong>: about two hours later, <strong>5,500 lines of code</strong> — placing and orchestrating polygon assets at (x,y,z) coordinates, writing the camera work and animation <strong>procedurally</strong>: a 3D movie that runs in the browser.</li>
    <li><strong>Karpathy's verdict</strong>: &ldquo;kind of janky but fun&rdquo; — and &ldquo;it's a bit <strong>mindboggling</strong>&rdquo; that the LLM had to place and choreograph all of it.</li>
</ul>

