<ul>
    <li><strong>Instructions get deleted.</strong> As models internalize behaviors, the &ldquo;do it this way&rdquo; layer depreciates — Cherny's 80% is the measured value of that depreciation.</li>
    <li><strong>Making gets cheap.</strong> In a world where one paragraph yields a 3D movie, the marginal cost of an artifact has fallen to $10 and two hours — Karpathy's experiment is the specimen.</li>
    <li><strong>So verification becomes the bottleneck.</strong> Karpathy's model audited itself by screenshots because it can't watch its own video; Cherny's answer is to have <strong>loops, not humans, run the verification</strong>. And last week OpenAI showing up with <a href="#!news-20260801-astra-math">Lean machine-checked certificates</a> for its mathematics is a different answer at the same spot — <strong>as generation becomes abundant, verification becomes precious</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>This meets the conclusion this wiki reached in <a href="#!news-20260728-software-factories-fail">&ldquo;The Harness Is Not Enough&rdquo;</a> — a harness's value lies not in the thickness of its instructions but in <strong>the quality of its verification loop</strong>. Cherny testified to that thesis as the harness's own author; Karpathy testified to it with the scale of the artifact.</p>

